  _Case("Acute pyelonephritis","Infectious disease","A 31-year-old woman has fever, rigors, dysuria and right flank pain.","Recent lower urinary symptoms; no known renal disease.","Fever, costovertebral angle tenderness and tachycardia.","Urinalysis and urine culture, pregnancy test where relevant, renal function and imaging if obstruction or complication suspected.","Systemic symptoms with flank tenderness support upper urinary tract infection.","Renal colic, appendicitis, pelvic inflammatory disease and musculoskeletal pain.","Assess sepsis and pregnancy, obtain urine culture, start guideline-concordant antibiotics and investigate obstruction or failure to improve.","Which finding best distinguishes pyelonephritis from uncomplicated cystitis?","Systemic illness with flank pain or costovertebral angle tenderness","Renal parenchymal involvement commonly produces fever, systemic symptoms and flank tenderness."),
  _Case("Acute pancreatitis","Gastroenterology","A 46-year-old develops severe persistent epigastric pain radiating to the back with vomiting.","Gallstones and recent heavy meal; alcohol history also reviewed.","Epigastric tenderness, tachycardia and reduced bowel sounds.","Lipase, liver tests, FBC, renal profile, calcium, triglycerides and ultrasound for biliary cause.","Typical pain plus lipase at least three times the upper reference limit supports acute pancreatitis.","Perforated ulcer, biliary colic, myocardial infarction and mesenteric ischaemia.","Assess severity and organ failure, provide monitored supportive care, identify biliary or metabolic cause and avoid routine early antibiotics without infection.","Which enzyme is preferred for biochemical diagnosis?","Serum lipase","Lipase is more specific and remains elevated longer than amylase in acute pancreatitis."),
  _Case("Acute cholangitis","Gastroenterology","A 67-year-old has fever, jaundice and right upper quadrant pain with rigors.","Known gallstones and intermittent biliary colic.","Fever, jaundice, right upper quadrant tenderness and hypotension developing.","Liver profile, blood cultures, ultrasound and MRCP or other imaging according to stability.","Charcot triad suggests ascending cholangitis; hypotension or confusion indicates severe disease.","Acute cholecystitis, hepatitis, pancreatitis and liver abscess.","Urgent sepsis care, IV antimicrobials and biliary decompression when indicated; involve gastroenterology early.","What is the key source-control intervention in obstructive acute cholangitis?","Urgent biliary drainage, commonly by ERCP","Antibiotics treat infection, but obstruction often requires decompression to achieve source control."),
  _Case("Inflammatory bowel disease flare","Gastroenterology","A 26-year-old has frequent bloody diarrhoea, urgency and cramping abdominal pain.","Known ulcerative colitis; recently stopped maintenance therapy.","Pallor, abdominal tenderness and tachycardia; assess distension and systemic toxicity.","FBC, CRP, electrolytes, stool culture and C. difficile testing; abdominal imaging if toxic megacolon suspected.","Bloody diarrhoea in known UC requires severity assessment and exclusion of infection.","Infectious colitis, Crohn disease, ischaemic colitis and colorectal neoplasia.","Assess severity, correct fluid and electrolyte deficits, involve gastroenterology and use evidence-based flare therapy after infection assessment.","Which complication must be considered with systemic toxicity and abdominal distension?","Toxic megacolon","Severe colitis can cause colonic dilatation with systemic toxicity and risk of perforation."),
  _Case("Bowel obstruction","Gastroenterology","A 70-year-old presents with colicky abdominal pain, distension, bilious vomiting and no flatus.","Previous abdominal surgery and known adhesions.","Distended tympanitic abdomen, high-pitched bowel sounds and dehydration.","FBC, electrolytes, lactate, renal function and CT abdomen/pelvis to define level, cause and complications.","Colic, vomiting, distension and obstipation suggest mechanical obstruction; look for strangulation.","Ileus, severe constipation, gastroenteritis and mesenteric ischaemia.","Resuscitate, keep nil by mouth, decompress when indicated, obtain urgent surgical review and monitor for ischaemia or perforation.","Which CT finding raises concern for strangulation or bowel ischaemia?","Reduced bowel-wall enhancement with mesenteric oedema or pneumatosis","Impaired perfusion and abnormal bowel-wall findings suggest threatened bowel requiring urgent surgical assessment."),
  _Case("Colorectal cancer presentation","Gastroenterology","A 69-year-old has altered bowel habit, fatigue and unintentional weight loss.","Family history of colorectal cancer in a first-degree relative.","Pallor and a palpable abdominal mass may be present; perform appropriate examination.","FBC and iron studies, FIT according to pathway, colonoscopy and staging imaging after diagnosis.","Iron-deficiency anaemia and altered bowel habit in an older adult require investigation for colorectal malignancy.","IBD, diverticular disease, haemorrhoids and dietary iron deficiency.","Urgent referral through the appropriate cancer pathway; confirm histology and stage in a multidisciplinary team.","Which anaemia pattern is commonly associated with occult right-sided colorectal bleeding?","Microcytic iron-deficiency anaemia","Chronic occult blood loss depletes iron stores and produces microcytosis."),
  _Case("Acute appendicitis","Surgery","A 21-year-old develops vague periumbilical pain that migrates to the right iliac fossa with anorexia.","No previous abdominal surgery; pain worsens with movement.","Localised right iliac fossa tenderness and guarding; assess pregnancy possibility.","FBC, CRP, urinalysis, pregnancy test when relevant and ultrasound or CT according to pathway.","Migration of pain reflects visceral-to-parietal peritoneal irritation and supports appendicitis.","Ectopic pregnancy, ovarian torsion, renal colic, gastroenteritis and mesenteric adenitis.","Surgical assessment, analgesia and fluids; imaging and antibiotics or appendicectomy according to diagnosis and local pathway.","Why does pain classically migrate from the umbilicus to the right iliac fossa?","Inflammation progresses from visceral afferent pain to parietal peritoneal irritation","Early visceral pain is poorly localised; later somatic peritoneal pain localises to the right lower quadrant."),
  _Case("Acute cholecystitis","Surgery","A 43-year-old woman has persistent right upper quadrant pain, fever and nausea after a fatty meal.","Previous episodic biliary colic.","Right upper quadrant tenderness and inspiratory arrest on palpation.","Liver profile, FBC, CRP and ultrasound for gallstones, wall thickening and pericholecystic fluid.","Persistent pain and inflammatory signs with gallbladder findings support acute cholecystitis.","Biliary colic, cholangitis, hepatitis, pancreatitis and peptic ulcer disease.","Analgesia, fluids, antibiotics when indicated and early surgical review for definitive management.","What does a positive Murphy sign indicate?","Inspiratory arrest due to pain when the inflamed gallbladder is palpated","The descending diaphragm brings the gallbladder into contact with the examiner's hand, provoking pain."),
  _Case("Perforated peptic ulcer","Surgery","A 57-year-old develops sudden severe epigastric pain that becomes generalised.","Regular NSAID use and previous dyspepsia.","Rigid abdomen, guarding, rebound tenderness and reduced bowel sounds.","Urgent upright chest radiograph or CT for free air, FBC, lactate, renal profile and cross-match.","Sudden pain with peritonism suggests perforation and requires immediate surgical assessment.","Pancreatitis, biliary disease, mesenteric ischaemia and ruptured aneurysm.","Resuscitation, nil by mouth, IV antimicrobials and proton-pump inhibitor as appropriate, urgent surgical source control.","What radiological sign may indicate a perforated hollow viscus?","Free intraperitoneal air under the diaphragm","Pneumoperitoneum in the appropriate clinical setting supports hollow-viscus perforation."),
  _Case("Acute limb ischaemia","Vascular surgery","A 74-year-old with atrial fibrillation develops sudden severe pain and pallor in the left leg.","Not taking prescribed anticoagulant; sudden onset at rest.","Cold pale limb, absent distal pulses, paraesthesia and reduced motor function.","Urgent vascular assessment, Doppler signals, ECG and CTA if it does not delay treatment.","Abrupt arterial occlusion causes the six Ps; sensory or motor deficit signals threatened limb.","DVT, chronic peripheral arterial disease, compartment syndrome and neuropathy.","Immediate vascular emergency pathway, anticoagulation when appropriate and urgent revascularisation assessment.","Which feature indicates a immediately threatened limb?","New motor weakness","Motor deficit is a sign of advanced ischaemia and requires immediate specialist intervention."),
  _Case("Compartment syndrome","Orthopaedics","A 24-year-old has escalating pain after a tibial fracture despite analgesia.","Tight cast applied after injury.","Tense swollen compartment, pain on passive stretch and evolving paraesthesia; pulses may remain present.","Clinical diagnosis; measure compartment pressures when uncertain, without delaying action in clear cases.","Disproportionate pain and pain on passive stretch are early warning signs of raised compartment pressure.","DVT, fracture pain, nerve injury and arterial occlusion.","Remove constriction, keep limb at heart level and obtain immediate orthopaedic review for urgent fasciotomy when indicated.","Does a palpable distal pulse exclude compartment syndrome?","No; pulses may remain present until late","Compartment pressure compromises microcirculation before large-artery pulses disappear."),
  _Case("Hip fracture in older adult","Orthopaedics","An 86-year-old falls from standing height and cannot bear weight because of hip pain.","Osteoporosis and frailty; previously mobile with a walking aid.","Shortened externally rotated leg with groin tenderness.","AP pelvis and lateral hip radiographs; MRI if occult fracture remains suspected despite negative radiographs.","Low-energy fall with deformity and inability to weight-bear strongly suggests proximal femoral fracture.","Pelvic fracture, dislocation, soft-tissue injury and referred spinal pain.","Analgesia, assess delirium and comorbidity, optimise promptly and coordinate orthogeriatric and surgical care.","Which complication is particularly associated with displaced intracapsular femoral neck fracture?","Avascular necrosis of the femoral head","Retinacular vessels may be disrupted, compromising femoral-head blood supply."),
  _Case("Cauda equina syndrome","Neurology","A 45-year-old with back pain develops bilateral sciatica, saddle numbness and difficulty initiating urination.","Recent worsening lumbar pain; no previous bladder dysfunction.","Reduced perineal sensation, altered anal tone and possible bilateral leg weakness.","Emergency MRI lumbar spine, bladder assessment and urgent spinal surgical review.","Saddle sensory loss and new bladder dysfunction are red flags for cauda equina compression.","Conus medullaris lesion, severe radiculopathy, spinal infection and pelvic pathology.","Treat as a time-critical spinal emergency; urgent imaging and decompression assessment.","Which symptom is a major red flag for cauda equina syndrome?","New urinary retention or altered bladder sensation","Compression of sacral roots can impair bladder function and perineal sensation."),
  _Case("Subarachnoid haemorrhage","Neurology","A 51-year-old experiences a thunderclap headache reaching maximal intensity within seconds.","No migraine history; vomiting and neck stiffness followed onset.","Photophobia, meningism and severe distress; focal deficit may be absent.","Urgent non-contrast CT head; further CSF or vascular imaging according to timing and specialist pathway.","Thunderclap onset is a red flag for subarachnoid bleeding even with a normal neurological examination.","Migraine, meningitis, reversible cerebral vasoconstriction and cervical artery dissection.","Emergency neurovascular assessment, blood pressure and complication management under specialist guidance.","What is the classic headache time course in aneurysmal SAH?","Sudden thunderclap headache reaching maximum intensity rapidly","Abrupt meningeal irritation from bleeding produces a severe headache with immediate or near-immediate peak."),
  _Case("Myasthenic crisis","Neurology","A 36-year-old with myasthenia develops worsening dysphagia, weak cough and breathlessness.","Known myasthenia gravis; recent respiratory infection.","Bulbar weakness, fatigability and shallow breathing; assess airway protection and respiratory effort.","Serial forced vital capacity and inspiratory force, blood gases when indicated and search for trigger.","Bulbar and respiratory muscle weakness may progress to ventilatory failure.","Cholinergic crisis, brainstem stroke, Guillain–Barré syndrome and botulism.","Urgent critical-care and neurology assessment, monitor respiratory function, protect airway and treat trigger; specialist immunotherapy.","Why can oxygen saturation remain deceptively normal early in myasthenic crisis?","Ventilatory muscle weakness and CO2 retention may precede hypoxaemia","Pulse oximetry alone does not measure ventilation; respiratory mechanics and clinical effort are essential."),
  _Case("Multiple sclerosis relapse","Neurology","A 29-year-old develops painful monocular visual loss and colour desaturation over two days.","Previous transient sensory episode that resolved; no fever or infection symptoms.","Relative afferent pupillary defect and reduced visual acuity in one eye.","MRI brain and orbits with appropriate sequences; exclude infection and alternative optic neuropathies.","Subacute painful optic neuritis with prior neurological episode suggests demyelination.","Ischaemic optic neuropathy, retinal disease, compressive lesion and infectious optic neuritis.","Neurology and ophthalmology assessment; manage disabling relapse according to specialist protocol and discuss disease-modifying therapy if indicated.","Which structure is commonly inflamed in demyelinating optic neuritis?","Optic nerve","Optic neuritis is an inflammatory demyelinating optic neuropathy frequently associated with MS."),
  _Case("Status epilepticus","Neurology","A 34-year-old has a generalised convulsion lasting more than five minutes without recovery.","Known epilepsy; missed antiseizure medication and febrile illness.","Ongoing tonic-clonic activity, hypoxia risk and reduced consciousness.","Bedside glucose, electrolytes, toxicology when indicated, blood gas and search for precipitant; EEG if non-convulsive status suspected.","A prolonged seizure or recurrent seizures without recovery constitutes status epilepticus.","Syncope, psychogenic events, hypoglycaemia and intracranial pathology.","Immediate ABC support, check glucose, administer emergency seizure treatment per protocol and escalate if refractory.","What time threshold is commonly used to trigger emergency treatment for convulsive status epilepticus?","Five minutes","A seizure lasting five minutes is unlikely to stop spontaneously and requires emergency treatment."),
  _Case("Thyroid storm","Endocrinology","A 38-year-old with untreated thyrotoxicosis develops fever, agitation, vomiting and severe tachycardia.","Stopped antithyroid medication; recent infection.","High fever, atrial fibrillation, tremor, agitation and possible heart failure.","Thyroid function tests, ECG, electrolytes, liver profile and assessment for precipitating illness.","Severe multisystem decompensation in thyrotoxicosis is a clinical emergency; hormone levels alone do not define storm.","Sepsis, serotonin syndrome, neuroleptic malignant syndrome and heat stroke.","Critical-care and endocrine management, supportive stabilisation and protocol-based multimodal therapy; treat precipitant.","Is thyroid storm diagnosed solely by an extremely high T4 concentration?","No; it is a clinical diagnosis of systemic decompensation","The severity of organ dysfunction and clinical context matter more than the absolute hormone concentration."),
  _Case("Myxoedema coma","Endocrinology","A 78-year-old is found confused and cold after several days of lethargy.","Known hypothyroidism with poor adherence; recent pneumonia.","Hypothermia, bradycardia, hypotension, hypoventilation and delayed reflex relaxation.","TSH, free T4, glucose, sodium, blood gas, ECG and infection evaluation.","Severe decompensated hypothyroidism can cause multisystem failure; coma is not required for the diagnosis.","Sepsis, adrenal crisis, stroke, sedative toxicity and hypothermia.","Emergency critical-care and endocrine treatment, supportive ventilation and cautious correction of metabolic abnormalities; consider adrenal coverage per protocol.","Which electrolyte disturbance is commonly associated with severe hypothyroidism?","Hyponatraemia","Impaired free-water clearance and reduced effective circulating volume can contribute to hyponatraemia."),
  _Case("Cushing syndrome","Endocrinology","A 41-year-old reports proximal muscle weakness, easy bruising and progressive central weight gain.","Long-term glucocorticoid exposure must be specifically reviewed.","Facial plethora, wide violaceous striae, proximal myopathy and hypertension.","Screen with validated cortisol testing after reviewing exogenous steroid use; then determine cause.","Characteristic catabolic skin and muscle findings with central adiposity suggest glucocorticoid excess.","Metabolic syndrome, depression, alcohol-related pseudo-Cushing and medication effects.","Confirm biochemical hypercortisolism, identify source and coordinate endocrine-directed treatment; never stop chronic steroids abruptly.","Which skin finding is particularly suggestive of endogenous Cushing syndrome?","Wide violaceous abdominal striae","Cortisol excess causes dermal thinning and impaired collagen, producing broad purple striae."),
  _Case("Phaeochromocytoma","Endocrinology","A 35-year-old has episodic pounding headache, palpitations, sweating and severe blood-pressure surges.","Episodes occur spontaneously; family history of endocrine tumours.","Paroxysmal hypertension, pallor, tremor and tachycardia.","Plasma free or urinary fractionated metanephrines; imaging after biochemical evidence.","Catecholamine spells suggest a catecholamine-secreting tumour.","Panic attacks, hyperthyroidism, stimulant use and arrhythmia.","Endocrine referral; preoperative alpha blockade before any beta blockade and specialist surgical planning.","Why should beta blockade not precede alpha blockade in a suspected functional tumour?","Unopposed alpha-adrenergic vasoconstriction can precipitate severe hypertension","Beta blockade first can leave alpha-mediated vasoconstriction unopposed."),
  _Case("Adrenal crisis","Endocrinology","A 44-year-old with known adrenal insufficiency presents with vomiting, abdominal pain and collapse.","Unable to retain replacement glucocorticoid during gastroenteritis.","Hypotension, dehydration, weakness and possible hyperpigmentation.","Immediate glucose, electrolytes, renal function and cortisol/ACTH if this does not delay treatment.","Acute illness with hypotension in adrenal insufficiency is adrenal crisis until treated.","Sepsis, DKA, haemorrhage and cardiogenic shock.","Immediate emergency glucocorticoid and isotonic fluid replacement according to crisis protocol; correct hypoglycaemia and precipitant.","Should blood sampling delay emergency hydrocortisone in suspected adrenal crisis?","No; treatment must not be delayed","Adrenal crisis is life-threatening; obtain samples only if this does not delay immediate therapy."),
  _Case("DKA with euglycaemia","Endocrinology","A 52-year-old taking an SGLT2 inhibitor presents with nausea, abdominal pain and deep breathing; glucose is only mildly elevated.","Recent fasting and reduced oral intake.","Dehydration, tachypnoea and abdominal tenderness.","Blood ketones, venous pH, bicarbonate, electrolytes, renal function and medication review.","Ketonaemia and metabolic acidosis can occur with near-normal glucose in SGLT2-associated euglycaemic DKA.","Starvation ketosis, lactic acidosis, toxic alcohols and sepsis.","Stop precipitating agent and manage as DKA with specialist protocol, including insulin, dextrose and electrolyte monitoring as indicated.","Which feature prevents exclusion of DKA when glucose is not markedly elevated?","Significant ketonaemia with metabolic acidosis","SGLT2 inhibitors can produce euglycaemic DKA; ketones and acid-base status are essential."),
  _Case("Nephritic syndrome","Nephrology","A 17-year-old develops cola-coloured urine, facial puffiness and reduced urine output.","Recent sore throat several weeks earlier.","Periorbital oedema and hypertension.","Urinalysis, urine microscopy, renal function, complement, streptococcal serology and protein quantification.","Haematuria, hypertension and impaired renal function indicate glomerular inflammation.","IgA nephropathy, lupus nephritis, vasculitis and hereditary nephritis.","Assess renal function and blood pressure, involve nephrology and investigate cause; manage fluid overload and complications.","Which urinary sediment finding strongly supports glomerular bleeding?","Red-cell casts","RBC casts form within renal tubules and localise bleeding to the nephron."),
  _Case("Nephrotic syndrome","Nephrology","A 32-year-old develops progressive periorbital and leg oedema with frothy urine.","No known diabetes; recent infection and medication history reviewed.","Generalised oedema, ascites and possible pleural effusion.","Urine protein quantification, serum albumin, lipid profile, renal function and cause-directed serology.","Heavy proteinuria with hypoalbuminaemia and oedema indicates nephrotic syndrome.","Heart failure, cirrhosis, malnutrition and isolated venous disease.","Investigate glomerular cause, manage oedema and thrombotic/infective risk, and arrange nephrology review.","Why does nephrotic syndrome increase venous thrombosis risk?","Urinary loss of anticoagulant proteins such as antithrombin","Loss of anticoagulant proteins and hepatic compensatory synthesis create a hypercoagulable state."),
  _Case("Rhabdomyolysis-associated AKI","Nephrology","A 27-year-old presents with muscle pain, weakness and dark urine after prolonged immobilisation.","Found unconscious after a long period on the floor.","Muscle tenderness and swelling; assess volume status and compartment syndrome.","Creatine kinase, potassium, phosphate, calcium, renal function and urinalysis for heme with few RBCs.","Myoglobin release from damaged muscle can cause tubular injury and electrolyte disturbances.","Haematuria, haemolysis, glomerulonephritis and drug-induced pigmenturia.","Urgent assessment, monitored fluid management tailored to cardiac and renal status, serial electrolytes and treatment of complications.","Why can urine dipstick be positive for blood despite few red cells on microscopy?","Myoglobin reacts with the dipstick heme reagent","The dipstick detects heme pigment and cannot distinguish myoglobin from haemoglobin or intact RBCs."),
  _Case("Renal artery stenosis","Nephrology","A 66-year-old develops resistant hypertension and a rise in creatinine after starting an ACE inhibitor.","Peripheral vascular disease and smoking history.","Abdominal bruit and difficult-to-control blood pressure.","Renal function trend, urinalysis and duplex ultrasound or CTA/MRA when appropriate.","Atherosclerotic renovascular disease can cause resistant hypertension and reduced filtration after RAAS blockade.","Essential hypertension, primary aldosteronism, CKD and medication non-adherence.","Review medication and renal function, assess cardiovascular risk and seek specialist advice on imaging and revascularisation indications.","Why can bilateral renal artery stenosis cause a marked creatinine rise after ACE inhibition?","Loss of angiotensin-II-mediated efferent arteriolar constriction","In reduced renal perfusion, efferent constriction helps maintain glomerular pressure; ACE inhibition removes this compensation."),
  _Case("Pre-eclampsia with severe features","Obstetrics","A 30-year-old at 34 weeks has headache, visual symptoms and elevated blood pressure.","Previously normotensive pregnancy; first pregnancy.","Severe-range hypertension, hyperreflexia and possible epigastric tenderness.","Urine protein assessment, FBC/platelets, liver enzymes, creatinine and fetal assessment.","New hypertension after 20 weeks with maternal organ features suggests pre-eclampsia with severe features.","Gestational hypertension, migraine, chronic hypertension and HELLP syndrome.","Urgent obstetric assessment, maternal stabilisation, seizure prophylaxis and blood-pressure control according to protocol; plan timing of birth.","Which symptom suggests possible cerebral involvement in severe pre-eclampsia?","Persistent severe headache or visual disturbance","Neurological symptoms may precede eclampsia and require urgent assessment and stabilisation."),
  _Case("Postpartum haemorrhage","Obstetrics","A 29-year-old develops heavy bleeding shortly after vaginal delivery.","Prolonged labour and uterine overdistension.","Heavy vaginal bleeding with a boggy enlarged uterus and tachycardia.","Quantify blood loss, FBC, coagulation, fibrinogen, cross-match and assess placenta and genital tract.","A boggy uterus suggests uterine atony, the commonest cause of primary PPH.","Retained placental tissue, genital tract trauma, coagulopathy and uterine inversion.","Activate obstetric haemorrhage protocol, resuscitate, uterine massage and uterotonics per protocol; identify and treat the cause.","What is the commonest cause of primary postpartum haemorrhage?","Uterine atony","Failure of the myometrium to contract adequately leaves placental-bed vessels open."),
  _Case("Shoulder dystocia","Obstetrics","During vaginal birth, the fetal head delivers but retracts against the perineum and shoulders fail to deliver.","Maternal diabetes and suspected fetal macrosomia.","Turtle sign with failure of restitution and shoulder delivery.","Clinical recognition is immediate; document timing and manoeuvres and assess neonatal and maternal injury afterwards.","The anterior shoulder is impacted behind the pubic symphysis; time-critical coordinated manoeuvres are required.","Breech presentation, cord prolapse and normal delayed restitution.","Call for help, use recognised first-line manoeuvres and avoid fundal pressure; escalate through the local shoulder dystocia algorithm.","Which action should be avoided during shoulder dystocia?","Fundal pressure","Fundal pressure can worsen impaction and increase maternal and fetal injury."),
  _Case("Eclampsia","Obstetrics","A 26-year-old at 36 weeks has a generalised seizure after severe headache and hypertension.","No known epilepsy; antenatal blood pressure recently elevated.","Postictal confusion, severe hypertension and possible hypoxia.","Maternal ABC assessment, glucose, FBC, platelets, renal/liver tests and fetal monitoring after stabilisation.","A seizure in a patient with pre-eclampsia is eclampsia until another cause is established.","Epilepsy, intracranial haemorrhage, cerebral venous thrombosis and hypoglycaemia.","Protect airway, treat seizure with magnesium sulphate according to protocol, control severe hypertension and plan birth after stabilisation.","Which medicine is first-line for prevention and treatment of eclamptic seizures?","Magnesium sulphate","Magnesium sulphate reduces recurrent eclamptic seizures and is preferred over routine anticonvulsants."),
  _Case("Testicular torsion","Urology","A 16-year-old develops sudden severe unilateral scrotal pain with nausea during sleep.","No urinary symptoms or trauma; onset was abrupt.","High-riding horizontal testis, swelling and absent cremasteric reflex.","Urgent clinical assessment; Doppler ultrasound only if diagnosis is uncertain and it will not delay surgery.","Sudden pain and abnormal testicular lie suggest spermatic-cord torsion; salvage is time-dependent.","Epididymo-orchitis, torsion of appendix testis, incarcerated hernia and trauma.","Immediate urological exploration when suspected; do not delay for imaging in a classic presentation.","Why is urgent surgical exploration required?","Testicular viability falls as duration of spermatic-cord ischaemia increases","Twisting compromises venous outflow and then arterial supply; prompt detorsion preserves viability."),
  _Case("Benign prostatic obstruction","Urology","A 72-year-old reports hesitancy, weak stream, nocturia and incomplete emptying.","Gradual symptoms over years; no fever or dysuria.","Palpable bladder may be present; smooth enlarged prostate on DRE.","Urinalysis, renal function, post-void residual and PSA discussion where appropriate.","Chronic voiding symptoms with smooth enlargement suggest benign prostatic obstruction.","Prostate cancer, urethral stricture, neurogenic bladder and UTI.","Assess complications and symptom burden, discuss behavioural and medical options and refer for retention, renal impairment or red flags.","Which symptom is most characteristic of bladder outlet obstruction?","Weak urinary stream with hesitancy","Mechanical obstruction increases resistance to urinary flow and causes voiding symptoms."),
  _Case("Diabetic retinopathy","Ophthalmology","A 55-year-old with long-standing diabetes reports blurred vision and floaters.","Poor glycaemic and blood-pressure control; missed retinal screening.","Fundoscopy may show microaneurysms, haemorrhages, exudates or neovascularisation.","Dilated retinal examination and retinal photography; OCT when macular oedema suspected.","Chronic hyperglycaemia damages retinal microvasculature; neovascularisation marks proliferative disease.","Hypertensive retinopathy, retinal vein occlusion, age-related macular degeneration and vitreous haemorrhage.","Prompt ophthalmology referral for sight-threatening disease and optimise systemic risk factors.","What drives proliferative diabetic retinopathy?","Retinal ischaemia causing VEGF-mediated neovascularisation","Capillary closure produces retinal hypoxia, stimulating VEGF and fragile new vessel growth."),
  _Case("Acute angle-closure glaucoma","Ophthalmology","A 64-year-old develops sudden painful red eye, blurred vision, halos and vomiting.","Hypermetropia; symptoms began in dim lighting.","Mid-dilated poorly reactive pupil, hazy cornea and markedly raised intraocular pressure.","Urgent tonometry and slit-lamp examination; assess fellow eye and medication triggers.","Painful red eye with halos and a fixed mid-dilated pupil is an ophthalmic emergency.","Anterior uveitis, keratitis, conjunctivitis and optic neuritis.","Immediate ophthalmology management to lower intraocular pressure and definitive angle treatment; avoid delay.","What pupillary appearance is typical of acute angle closure?","A mid-dilated poorly reactive pupil","Iris bombe and angle obstruction impair aqueous outflow, producing a mid-dilated pupil and rapid pressure rise."),
  _Case("Iron-deficiency anaemia","Haematology","A 35-year-old reports fatigue, exertional dyspnoea and pica.","Heavy menstrual bleeding and low dietary iron intake.","Pallor, koilonychia and flow murmur may be present.","FBC, ferritin, transferrin saturation and investigate source of blood loss.","Microcytosis with depleted ferritin supports iron deficiency; identify the cause rather than treating the number alone.","Thalassaemia trait, anaemia of chronic disease, sideroblastic anaemia and lead toxicity.","Replace iron appropriately and investigate menstrual or gastrointestinal blood loss according to age and risk.","Which laboratory result best reflects depleted iron stores?","Low serum ferritin","Ferritin is the most useful routine marker of iron stores, although inflammation can elevate it."),
  _Case("Acute haemolytic transfusion reaction","Haematology","A patient develops fever, flank pain, hypotension and dark urine shortly after a blood transfusion begins.","Recently transfused for surgical blood loss.","Fever, tachycardia, hypotension and haemoglobinuria.","Stop transfusion, maintain IV access, notify transfusion service; check clerical identity, DAT, haemolysis markers, renal function and coagulation.","Acute intravascular haemolysis soon after transfusion suggests major incompatibility until excluded.","Febrile non-haemolytic reaction, sepsis, TRALI, TACO and allergic reaction.","Stop the transfusion immediately, provide emergency supportive care and follow transfusion-reaction protocol.","What is the first action when an acute haemolytic transfusion reaction is suspected?","Stop the transfusion immediately","Further incompatible red-cell exposure can rapidly worsen haemolysis and shock."),
  _Case("Disseminated intravascular coagulation","Haematology","A septic patient develops oozing from cannula sites, bruising and worsening organ dysfunction.","Severe gram-negative sepsis and shock.","Petechiae, bleeding, cool peripheries and possible acral ischaemia.","Platelets, PT/INR, aPTT, fibrinogen, D-dimer, blood film and serial trends.","Systemic coagulation activation consumes platelets and factors while generating microthrombi.","TTP, severe liver disease, isolated thrombocytopenia and anticoagulant effect.","Treat the underlying trigger, support haemostasis for clinically significant bleeding or procedures and monitor serial coagulation results.","Which laboratory pattern is typical of consumptive DIC?","Thrombocytopenia, prolonged coagulation times, raised D-dimer and reduced fibrinogen","Widespread thrombin generation consumes platelets and clotting factors and increases fibrin breakdown."),
  _Case("Sickle cell acute chest syndrome","Haematology","A 22-year-old with sickle cell disease develops fever, chest pain, cough and hypoxaemia.","Recent vaso-occlusive pain episode and dehydration.","Fever, tachypnoea, crackles and oxygen saturation below baseline.","Chest radiograph showing a new infiltrate, FBC, reticulocytes, cultures and oxygenation assessment.","A new pulmonary infiltrate with respiratory symptoms or fever defines acute chest syndrome.","Pneumonia, pulmonary embolism, asthma and fluid overload.","Urgent multidisciplinary care, oxygen as needed, analgesia, incentive spirometry, infection assessment and transfusion consideration per severity.","What radiographic feature is required for the diagnosis of acute chest syndrome?","A new pulmonary infiltrate","A new infiltrate with compatible symptoms distinguishes acute chest syndrome from uncomplicated pain crisis."),
  _Case("Anaphylaxis","Emergency medicine","A 24-year-old develops wheeze, urticaria, lip swelling and collapse minutes after a bee sting.","Previous severe sting reaction.","Hypotension, stridor, wheeze and generalised urticaria.","Clinical diagnosis; obtain tryptase at appropriate intervals after stabilisation without delaying treatment.","Rapid multi-system involvement after exposure indicates anaphylaxis.","Severe asthma, vasovagal syncope, panic attack and isolated urticaria.","Immediate IM adrenaline in the anterolateral thigh, call for help, position safely and provide ABC support; repeat per protocol if needed.","Which route and site are preferred for first-line adrenaline?","Intramuscular injection into the anterolateral thigh","IM thigh administration provides rapid reliable absorption and is first-line in anaphylaxis."),
  _Case("Acute ischaemic stroke","Emergency medicine","A 67-year-old suddenly develops facial droop, arm weakness and dysarthria 50 minutes ago.","Atrial fibrillation and hypertension; establish exact last-known-well time.","Focal neurological deficit; check glucose and assess airway, swallowing and blood pressure.","Urgent non-contrast CT, CT angiography when indicated, glucose and stroke severity assessment.","Sudden focal deficit suggests stroke; imaging distinguishes haemorrhage and identifies large-vessel occlusion.","Hypoglycaemia, seizure with Todd paresis, migraine and intracranial haemorrhage.","Activate stroke pathway; assess reperfusion eligibility and thrombectomy according to imaging, timing and current criteria.","Why must non-contrast CT be obtained urgently before IV thrombolysis?","To exclude intracranial haemorrhage","Thrombolysis in haemorrhagic stroke can worsen bleeding catastrophically."),
  _Case("Acute upper GI bleed","Emergency medicine","A 61-year-old presents with melaena, coffee-ground vomiting and presyncope.","Peptic ulcer history and regular NSAID use.","Tachycardia, postural hypotension and pallor; assess shock and liver disease.","FBC, urea/creatinine, coagulation, liver profile, group-and-crossmatch and urgent endoscopy after resuscitation.","Melaena and haemodynamic compromise indicate significant upper GI bleeding.","Variceal haemorrhage, lower GI bleeding, iron therapy and swallowed blood.","Resuscitate, review anticoagulants, use risk assessment and arrange endoscopy; suspected varices require specific early therapy.","Why may the blood urea rise disproportionately in upper GI bleeding?","Digestion and absorption of blood protein increases urea production","The nitrogen load from digested blood increases hepatic urea generation."),
  _Case("Heat stroke","Emergency medicine","A 32-year-old collapses during strenuous exercise in extreme heat with confusion.","Prolonged exertion and inadequate fluid replacement.","Marked hyperthermia, altered mental status, tachycardia and possible seizures.","Core temperature, electrolytes, renal/liver function, CK, coagulation and ECG.","Central nervous system dysfunction with severe hyperthermia indicates heat stroke.","Heat exhaustion, sepsis, serotonin syndrome and neuroleptic malignant syndrome.","Immediate rapid active cooling, ABC support and monitoring for rhabdomyolysis, renal injury and coagulopathy.","What distinguishes heat stroke from heat exhaustion?","Central nervous system dysfunction in the setting of hyperthermia","Confusion, seizures or coma indicate failure of thermoregulation with organ injury."),
  _Case("Diabetic foot infection","Surgery","A 59-year-old with diabetes has a plantar ulcer with surrounding erythema and purulent discharge.","Peripheral neuropathy and poor glycaemic control; ulcer present for weeks.","Warmth, erythema, purulence and reduced protective sensation; assess pulses and systemic toxicity.","Probe-to-bone when appropriate, deep tissue culture after cleaning/debridement, inflammatory markers and imaging for osteomyelitis.","Infected ulcer in a neuropathic foot requires severity grading and assessment for deep infection and ischaemia.","Charcot arthropathy, gout, pressure injury and venous ulcer.","Urgent multidisciplinary foot care, offloading, source control, antibiotics based on severity and assessment of perfusion.","Why is a superficial swab often less useful than a deep tissue specimen?","It may reflect colonising surface flora rather than the invasive pathogen","A properly obtained deep specimen better represents organisms causing tissue infection."),
  _Case("Acute urinary retention","Urology","A 75-year-old man has painful inability to pass urine for ten hours.","Known lower urinary tract symptoms; recently started an anticholinergic medicine.","Distended tender bladder and suprapubic dullness.","Bladder scan, urinalysis, renal function and medication review.","Acute painful inability to void with a distended bladder indicates retention.","Anuria from renal failure, ureteric obstruction, constipation and neurogenic bladder.","Prompt bladder decompression when appropriate, identify precipitant, monitor post-obstructive diuresis and arrange follow-up.","Which bedside test rapidly confirms bladder distension?","Bladder ultrasound scan","A bladder scan estimates retained urine and helps distinguish retention from low urine production."),
  _Case("Pre-renal AKI","Nephrology","A 70-year-old develops oliguria after several days of vomiting and diarrhoea.","Taking diuretics and an ACE inhibitor; poor oral intake.","Dry mucosa, postural hypotension and low JVP.","Creatinine trend, electrolytes, urinalysis, urine output and assessment for obstruction.","Reduced effective circulating volume lowers renal perfusion and glomerular filtration.","Acute tubular injury, obstruction, glomerulonephritis and interstitial nephritis.","Restore perfusion with careful reassessment, review contributing medicines and monitor urine output, creatinine and potassium.","Which urine sediment is often seen in uncomplicated pre-renal AKI?","Relatively bland sediment with hyaline casts","Pre-renal physiology does not initially cause substantial tubular epithelial injury."),
  _Case("Systemic lupus nephritis","Rheumatology","A 24-year-old woman with photosensitive rash develops oedema, hypertension and foamy urine.","Known SLE with arthralgia and oral ulcers.","Malar rash, peripheral oedema and hypertension.","Urinalysis, protein quantification, renal function, complement, anti-dsDNA and renal biopsy when indicated.","Proteinuria or active urinary sediment in SLE raises concern for immune-complex nephritis; biopsy class guides treatment.","Drug-induced nephritis, infection, diabetic kidney disease and other glomerulopathies.","Urgent nephrology/rheumatology assessment, quantify renal involvement and use biopsy-guided immunosuppression.","Which complement pattern commonly accompanies active immune-complex lupus nephritis?","Reduced C3 and C4","Complement is consumed by immune-complex activation during active disease."),
  _Case("Gout flare","Rheumatology","A 54-year-old develops abrupt severe pain, redness and swelling of the first metatarsophalangeal joint overnight.","Chronic kidney disease and thiazide use; previous similar episodes.","Hot exquisitely tender monoarthritis with restricted movement.","Joint aspiration for crystals and culture when diagnosis uncertain or infection possible; serum urate is not diagnostic during flare.","Needle-shaped negatively birefringent monosodium urate crystals support gout.","Septic arthritis, CPPD, trauma and cellulitis.","Exclude septic arthritis, treat flare according to renal function and comorbidities, then address urate-lowering prevention when indicated.","What is the characteristic crystal appearance in gout?","Needle-shaped, strongly negatively birefringent crystals","Monosodium urate crystals are needle-shaped and show negative birefringence under polarised light."),
  _Case("Rheumatoid arthritis","Rheumatology","A 39-year-old has symmetrical painful swollen MCP and PIP joints with morning stiffness lasting two hours.","Symptoms for four months; fatigue and family history of autoimmune disease.","Soft synovitis of MCP/PIP joints with reduced grip; DIP joints relatively spared.","ESR/CRP, rheumatoid factor, anti-CCP and baseline hand/foot imaging.","Persistent symmetrical small-joint synovitis with prolonged morning stiffness suggests inflammatory RA.","Osteoarthritis, psoriatic arthritis, viral arthritis and SLE.","Early rheumatology referral and treat-to-target DMARD strategy after diagnosis and safety screening.","Which antibody is more specific for rheumatoid arthritis and predicts erosive disease?","Anti-cyclic citrullinated peptide antibody","Anti-CCP has high specificity and is associated with more erosive disease."),
  _Case("Systemic sclerosis renal crisis","Rheumatology","A 48-year-old with diffuse systemic sclerosis develops abrupt severe hypertension, headache and acute kidney injury.","Recent rapid skin thickening; glucocorticoid exposure reviewed.","Severe hypertension, retinal changes and possible microangiopathic features.","Serial creatinine, urinalysis, blood film, platelets and haemolysis markers.","Abrupt hypertension and AKI in diffuse scleroderma suggests renal crisis.","TTP, malignant hypertension from other causes, glomerulonephritis and drug toxicity.","Emergency specialist management with prompt ACE-inhibitor therapy under monitoring and assessment for microangiopathy.","Which drug class is central to treatment of scleroderma renal crisis?","ACE inhibitor","ACE inhibition improves outcomes and should be initiated promptly despite an initial creatinine rise being possible."),
  _Case("Acute bacterial meningitis","Infectious disease","A 20-year-old presents with fever, headache, photophobia and neck stiffness.","No prior antibiotics; immunisation status uncertain.","Fever, meningism and altered attention; assess for focal deficit, seizure and shock.","Blood cultures, FBC, CRP, glucose and LP when safe; imaging first only when indicated by clinical red flags.","Meningeal inflammation with systemic illness requires urgent empiric treatment and organism-directed investigation.","Encephalitis, subarachnoid haemorrhage, migraine and brain abscess.","Do not delay empiric antibiotics for imaging or LP when unsafe; provide isolation and urgent specialist care.","Which CSF pattern is typical of untreated bacterial meningitis?","Neutrophilic pleocytosis, high protein and low CSF glucose","Bacterial metabolism and impaired glucose transport lower CSF glucose while inflammation raises protein and neutrophils."),
  _Case("HELLP syndrome","Obstetrics","A 32-year-old at 33 weeks develops right upper quadrant pain, nausea and malaise with hypertension.","Recent pre-eclampsia symptoms; no known liver disease.","Epigastric/RUQ tenderness, hypertension and possible bruising or bleeding.","FBC with platelets, AST/ALT, LDH, bilirubin, blood film, renal function and coagulation.","Haemolysis, elevated liver enzymes and low platelets define HELLP and can occur without dramatic proteinuria.","Acute fatty liver of pregnancy, viral hepatitis, TTP and gallbladder disease.","Urgent obstetric and critical-care assessment, maternal stabilisation, blood product support if needed and delivery planning.","What do the letters HELLP represent?","Haemolysis, elevated liver enzymes and low platelets","The acronym describes the key laboratory triad of this severe pregnancy complication."),
];

